er line L3: (2,3)-(5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, allowed. So L3: (2,3)-(5,1). This covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,4),(3,1),(4,2). Let's try line L4: (2,4)-(4,2) slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nTry line L4: (2,4)-(3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, allowed. So L4: (2,4)-(3,1). This covers (2,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered all points except (4,2). Let's see if any line already covers (4,2). L1: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1, does it pass through (4,2)? Equation: y-1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x-1. For x=4, y=3, not 2. So no. L2: (2,2)-(3,3) slope 1, equation: y-2 = 1*(x-2) => y = x. For x=4, y=4, not 2. L3: (2,3)-(5,1) slope -2/3, equation: y-3 = -2/3 (x-2) => y = 3 - (2/3)(x-2). For x=4, y = 3 - (2/3)*2 = 3 - 4/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not 2. L4: (2,4)-(3,1) slope -3, equation: y-4 = -3 (x-2) => y = 4 - 3(x-2) = 4 - 3x + 6 = 10 - 3x. For x=4, y = 10 - 12 = -2, not 2. So (4,2) is uncovered.\n\nThus we need to adjust to cover (4,2). Could we modify L1 or L2 to also include (4,2)? Let's see if we can have a line that includes (2,1),(3,2),(4,2). Points (2,1) and (3,2) define slope 1. Does (4,2) lie on that line? For slope 1 line through (2,1): y = x-1. At x=4, y=3, not 2. So no.\n\nWhat about line through (2,2),(3,3),(4,2)? Points (2,2) and (3,3) slope 1, line y = x. At x=4, y=4, not 2. So no.\n\nWhat about line through (2,3),(3,2),(4,2)? Points (2,3) and (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nWhat about line through (2,4),(3,2),(4,2)? (2,4)-(3,2) slope = -2, line equation: y-4 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0, not 2. So no.\n\nWhat about line through (2,4),(3,1),(4,2)? (2,4)-(3,1) slope = -3, line eq: y-4 = -3(x-2) => y = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2, not 2.\n\nThus (4,2) cannot be covered by any of the lines we have. Could we replace one of the lines with a line that covers (4,2) and still cover its previous points? Let's try to incorporate (4,2) into L3 maybe. L3 currently (2,3)-(5,1). Could