ny line x+y=n+1 covering C, E, F. Then we have left A, B, D. Use sunny line L1: A and D? That's horizontal, not allowed. A and B vertical, not allowed. B and D slope -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus seems impossible to cover the six points with one non-sunny line and two sunny lines. But we haven't exhausted all possibilities. Let's systematically search for a covering of the six points with 1 non-sunny line (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) and 2 sunny lines (any slope not 0, undefined, -1). The non-sunny line can be any of the following types: vertical x = constant (i = n-2, n-1, n), horizontal y = constant (b = 1,2,3), diagonal x+y = constant (c = n, n+1, n-1?). Let's list all possible non-sunny lines that intersect the set of six points:\n\n- Vertical x = n-2: covers A, B, C.\n- Vertical x = n-1: covers D, E.\n- Vertical x = n: covers F only.\n- Horizontal y = 1: covers A, D, F.\n- Horizontal y = 2: covers B, E.\n- Horizontal y = 3: covers C only.\n- Diagonal x+y = n-1: passes through? Points with sum n-1: (n-2,? ) sum = n-1 => (n-2,? ) => b = (n-1)-(n-2) = 1 => (n-2,1) = A. Also (n-1,0) not allowed (b≥1). So only A. Also maybe (n-3,2) not in rightmost set. So diagonal x+y=n-1 covers only A among the six.\n- Diagonal x+y = n: covers B (n-2,2) and D (n-1,1). Also maybe (n-3,3) not in set.\n- Diagonal x+y = n+1: covers C (n-2,3), E (n-1,2), F (n,1). Also maybe (n-3,4) not in set.\n- Diagonal x+y = n+2: covers? (n-2,4) not in set (b max 3). So no.\n\nThus the possible non-sunny lines covering subsets of the six points are:\n\n- V1: x=n-2 covering {A,B,C}\n- V2: x=n-1 covering {D,E}\n- V3: x=n covering {F}\n- H1: y=1 covering {A,D,F}\n- H2: y=2 covering {B,E}\n- H3: y=3 covering {C}\n- D1: x+y=n covering {B,D}\n- D2: x+y=n+1 covering {C,E,F}\n- D0: x+y=n-1 covering {A}\n\nWe need to choose exactly one of these non-sunny lines (since we have only one non-sunny line left) and then cover the remaining points with two sunny lines. The sunny lines can be any lines with slopes not 0, undefined, -1. They can pass through any points, including points already covered by other lines. So we need to see if there exists a selection of a non-sunny line L0 from the above list such that the remaining points (the six points minus those covered by L0) can be covered by two sunny lines.\n\nLet's denote the set of points S = {A,B,C,D,E,F}. For each possible L0, compute the uncovered set U = S \\ L0. Then ask: can U be covered by two sunny lines? That means we need to partition U into at most two subsets each of which is collinear with a sunny slope (i.e., there exists a line with slope not 0, undefined, -1 passing through all points in that subset). The subsets can be of size 1 (a line through a single point is trivially any line, but we need to ensure the line is sunny; we can always choose a sunny line through a single point with any slope not forbidden). So we can always cover a single point with a sunny line. So the question reduces to: can we cover U with at most two sunny lines, i.e., can we partition U into at most two subsets each of which is collinear with a sunny slope? Since we can always cover a single point with a sunny line, the worst-case scenario is that we need as many sunny lines as points. So we need to see if we can cover U with ≤2 lines.\n\nThus we need to see if U can be covered by two lines, each of which is not forbidden. So we need to see if U can be partitioned into at most two collinear subsets (with allowed slopes). Let's examine each L0.\n\nCase 1: L0 = V1 (x=n-2) covering {A,B,C}. Then U = {D,E,F}. As we saw earlier, any pair among D,E,F is non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/diagonal). So we cannot cover any pair with a sunny line. So we would need three sunny lines (one for each point). So fails.\n\nCase 2: L0 = V2 (x=n-1) covering {D,E}. Then U = {A,B,C,F}. Check possible sunny pairs among these four points:\n\n- A (n-2,1) and B (n-2,2): vertical, not allowed.\n- A and C: vertical, not allowed.\n- A and F: horizontal, not allowed.\n- B and C: vertical, not allowed.\n- B and F: slope (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = -1/(2) = -0.5, allowed (sunny). So B-F can be covered by a sunny line.\n- C and F: slope (1-3)/(2) = -1, not allowed.\nThus we can pair B and F in one sunny line. Then remaining points are A and C. A and C vertical, not allowed. So we would need separate sunny lines for A and C, total 3 sunny lines. So fails.\n\nCase 3: L0 = V3 (x=n) covering {F}. Then U = {A,B,C,D,E}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- A-B vertical, not allowed.\n- A-C vertical, not allowed.\n- A-D horizontal, not allowed.\n- A-E slope 1, allowed.\n- B-C vertical, not allowed.\n- B-D slope -1, not allowed.\n- B-E horizontal, not allowed.\n- C-D slope -2, allowed.\n- C-E slope -1, not allowed.\n- D-E vertical, not allowed.\n\nThus we have possible sunny pairs: (A,E) and (C,D). So we can cover A and E with one sunny line, and C and D with another sunny line. That leaves B uncovered. So we would need a third sunny line for B. So fails.\n\nCase 4: L0 = H1 (y=1) covering {A,D,F}. Then U = {B,C,E}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- B-C vertical, not allowed.\n- B-E horizontal, not allowed.\n- C-E slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus all pairs are non-sunny, need three sunny lines. Fail.\n\nCase 5: L0 = H2 (y=2) covering {B,E}. Then U = {A,C,D,F}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- A-C vertical, not allowed.\n- A-D horizontal, not allowed.\n- A-F horizontal, not allowed.\n- C-D slope -2, allowed.\n- C-F slope -1, not allowed.\n- D-F horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus we have one possible sunny pair: (C,D). Then remaining points A and F are left, but A-F horizontal, not allowed. So need separate sunny lines for A and F, total 3 sunny lines. Fail.\n\nCase 6: H3 (y=3) covering {C}. Then U = {A,B,D,E,F}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- A-B vertical, not allowed.\n- A-D horizontal, not allowed.\n- A-E slope 1, allowed.\n- A-F horizontal, not allowed.\n- B-D slope -1, not allowed.\n- B-E horizontal, not allowed.\n- B-F slope -0.5, allowed.\n- D-E vertical, not allowed.\n- D-F horizontal, not allowed.\n- E-F slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we have possible sunny pairs: (A,E) and (B,F). That's two pairs. That leaves D uncovered. D cannot be paired with any of the remaining points (A,E,B,F) with a sunny line: D-A horizontal, D-B slope -1, D-E vertical, D-F horizontal. All not allowed. So D would need its own sunny line, total 3 sunny lines. Fail.\n\nCase 7: D1 (x+y=n) covering {B,D}. Then U = {A,C,E,F}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- A-C vertical, not allowed.\n- A-E slope 1, allowed.\n- A-F horizontal, not allowed.\n- C-E slope -1, not allowed.\n- C-F slope -1, not allowed.\n- E-F slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we have one possible sunny pair: (A,E). Then remaining points C and F are left, but C-F slope -1, not allowed. So need separate sunny lines for C and F, total 3 sunny lines. Fail.\n\nCase 8: D2 (x+y=n+1) covering {C,E,F}. Then U = {A,B,D}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- A-B vertical, not allowed.\n- A-D horizontal, not allowed.\n- B-D slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus all pairs non-sunny, need three sunny lines. Fail.\n\nCase 9: D0 (x+y=n-1) covering {A}. Then U = {B,C,D,E,F}. Check possible sunny pairs:\n\n- B-C vertical, not allowed.\n- B-D slope -1, not allowed.\n- B-E horizontal, not allowed.\n- B-F slope -0.5, allowed.\n- C-D slope -2, allowed.\n- C-E slope -1, not allowed.\n- C-F slope -1, not allowed.\n- D-E vertical, not allowed.\n- D-F horizontal, not allowed.\n- E-F slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we have possible sunny pairs: (B,F) and (C,D). That's two pairs, leaving E uncovered. E cannot be paired with any of the remaining points (A,B,C,D,F) with a sunny line? Let's check E with A: slope (1-2)/(n-2-? ) Actually A is (n-2,1). E is (n-1,2). Slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1, allowed. Wait, we already used A? Actually A is not in U (since we used D0 covering A). So A is covered by non-sunny line, but we can still use A in a sunny line? Yes, we can use any point, even if already covered. So w